Na krunicu se nastavljaju **Litanije Božjem milosrđu**. Počinju klasičnim zazivima „Gospodine, smiluj se“ i „Kriste, čuj nas“ te zazivima Oca, Sina Otkupitelja svijeta, Duha Svetoga i Svetoga Trojstva. Potom slijedi dugi niz zaziva koji prikazuju milosrđe u svim njegovim pojavnim oblicima. Na svaki se odgovara istim izrazom pouzdanja: **„uzdam se u tebe“**.

Litanije milosrđe opisuju kao „neshvatljivu tajnu Presvetoga Trojstva“ i „izraz njegove najveće moći“. Pritom kao da prate cijelu povijest spasenja:

- **stvaranje**: milosrđe koje se objavilo u stvaranju nebeskih duhova i koje je čovjeka „iz ništavila pozvalo u život“;
- **otkupljenje**: milosrđe koje štiti od zasluženih kazni, oslobađa od bijede grijeha i izlijeva se „iz rana Kristovih“ i iz Presvetoga Srca;
- **Crkva i sakramenti**: milosrđe vidljivo u ustanovljenju Crkve „koja obuhvaća cijeli svijet“, neograničeno u sakramentu krštenja i pokore, neizmjerno u sakramentu oltara i svećeništva;
- **ljudska patnja**: milosrđe kao vrelo bolesnicima i patnicima, utjeha onima „skršena srca“, ufanje onih koji očajavaju i mir umirućih;
- **vječnost**: milosrđe koje čuva od paklenoga ognja, olakšava patnje dušama u čistilištu te je „slast i neizmjerna radost sviju svetih“.

Litanije završavaju psalmskim izričajem: **„Bog je milosrdan i pun samilosti, spor na srdžbu i pun dobrote. Zato ću dovijeka klicati milosrđu Božjemu.“**

Zaključna molitva sažima njihovu poruku. Od Boga, čije je milosrđe „neistraživo“, a blago smilovanja „neiscrpivo“, traži se da umnoži milosrđe u ljudima, kako u teškim trenucima ne bi očajavali ni bili obeshrabreni. Moli se da se s velikim pouzdanjem predaju njegovoj svetoj volji, „koja je sama ljubav i smilovanje“. Molitva se upućuje po Isusu Kristu, kojeg naziva **„kraljem milosrđa“**.

sections.content_en:
The Rosary is followed by the **Litany of Divine Mercy**. It begins with the classic invocations “Lord, have mercy” and “Christ, hear us”, and invocations of the Father, the Son Redeemer of the world, the Holy Spirit, and the Holy Trinity. Then follows a long series of invocations depicting mercy in all its manifestations. Each is answered with the same expression of trust: **“I trust in you”**.

The Litany describes mercy as an “incomprehensible mystery of the Most Holy Trinity” and an “expression of His greatest power”. In doing so, it seems to trace the entire history of salvation:

- **creation**: mercy that revealed itself in the creation of heavenly spirits and that called man “out of nothingness into life”;
- **redemption**: mercy that shields from deserved punishments, frees from the misery of sin, and pours forth “from the wounds of Christ” and from the Sacred Heart;
- **the Church and the sacraments**: mercy visible in the institution of the Church “which encompasses the whole world”, boundless in the sacrament of baptism and penance, immeasurable in the sacrament of the altar and the priesthood;
- **human suffering**: mercy as a fountain for the sick and suffering, consolation to the “brokenhearted”, hope of those who despair, and peace of the dying;
- **eternity**: mercy that preserves from the fire of hell, relieves the sufferings of the souls in purgatory, and is the “delight and immense joy of all the saints”.

The Litany concludes with a psalmic expression: **“God is merciful and full of compassion, slow to anger and full of goodness. Therefore I will forever acclaim the mercy of God.”**

The closing prayer summarizes their message. God, whose mercy is “inscrutable” and whose treasury of compassion is “inexhaustible”, is asked to multiply mercy in people, so that in difficult moments they may neither despair nor be discouraged. It is prayed that they surrender themselves with great trust to His holy will, “which is love and mercy itself”. The prayer is offered through Jesus Christ, whom it calls the **“King of Mercy”**.
